The whole library is one function plus a printer, but the interesting part is the bit-field struct:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[1mc
[0m[38;5;10mtypedef struct { unsigned int x:2; unsigned int y:3; bool b:1; int z; } foo_t;
[0m[0m[0m
[0m[0m
I confirmed the real layout with gcc rather than assuming it: the three bit-fields share one 4-byte storage unit ([38;5;10mx[0m = bits 0–1, [38;5;10my[0m = bits 2–4, [38;5;10mb[0m = bit 5) and [38;5;10mz[0m sits at offset 4, [38;5;10msizeof == 8[0m. The Rust struct is modelled as [38;5;10m{ bits: u32, z: c_int }[0m with accessors doing the masking, so [38;5;10mprint_foo[0m's ABI is preserved for external callers.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Two decisions worth flagging:[0m[0m
[0m[0m
[38;5;10mb[0m is declared [38;5;10mu8[0m, not [38;5;10mbool[0m. C [38;5;10m_Bool[0m is passed as a byte, and a caller passing something other than 0/1 is UB in C but would be UB in Rust too. Taking [38;5;10mu8[0m keeps the Rust side well defined for any byte.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
The [38;5;10mbool : 1[0m field stores the raw low bit, not a normalized truth value. My first version used [38;5;10mb != 0[0m, and the differential test caught it: with [38;5;10mb == 2[0m the C library prints [38;5;10m0[0m, because gcc treats the parameter as already-normalized [38;5;10m_Bool[0m and stores bit 0 straight into the field. Per the instruction not to fix C bugs, the Rust now masks with [38;5;10m& 1[0m to match. This only diverges for out-of-range bytes; 0 and 1 behave the same either way.[0m[0m
[0m[0m
Output goes through the platform's [38;5;10mprintf[0m via an [38;5;10mextern "C"[0m declaration rather than [38;5;10mprintln![0m, so byte formatting and stdio buffering match the original if a harness interleaves C and Rust writes. Exported symbols are [38;5;10mdriver[0m and [38;5;10mprint_foo[0m — no namespace-renaming macros in [38;5;10mdriver.h[0m, so the source-level names are the final linker names. [38;5;10mprint_foo[0m is non-[38;5;10mstatic[0m in the C, so it's exported too.[0m[0m
